  "operation": {
    "name": "lowercase hexadecimal encoding (bounded variant)",
    "contract": "out[2*i] = table[in[i] >> 4], out[2*i+1] = table[in[i] & 0xf] for table = \"0123456789abcdef\", applied to the first (length & 0x3f) input bytes: (length & 0x30) bytes in the vector loop and (length & 0xf) in the scalar tail; exact for length <= 63; input rdi, length rsi, output rdx; no return value",
    "evidence": [
      "the body's only external references are the same two constants the F1 encoder reads: a 16x0x0f nibble mask and the 16-byte ASCII table \"0123456789abcdef\" in .rodata",
      "the vector loop is the F1 loop's shape: it splits each 16-byte block into nibbles, shuffles both nibble planes through the table with pshufb, interleaves the characters and stores 32 bytes per 16 input bytes",
      "the entry chunking is the bounded part: the vector byte count is length & 0x30 (at most 48, three chunks) instead of F1's length & ~0xf, and the remainder is length & 0xf, so bits of the length above bit 5 select nothing",
      "the scalar tail converts the remainder with the same table, hi nibble first: a two-byte loop over remainder & 0xe whose output base is derived as (2 * length) & 0x60 = 2 * (length & 0x30), plus one final byte when the remainder is odd",
      "the single selecting consumer passes only the length constants 32 and 2, both below 64, so the modulo-64 truncation never fires in the use the binary makes of this body"
    ]
  },
